{"ts": 1789784136.0513742, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Submitted specification\n\n**Long f10, long f8, short f38** \u2014 in-sample Sharpe **1.396** (n=5000), against a search-adjusted bar of 1.019.\n\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe: 1.05 \u00b1 0.30**\n\n### How I got there\n\nI screened all 40 features individually, then built combinations around the leaders rather than searching the full ~10k specification space \u2014 an exhaustive max-picking search would have inflated the winner's curse badly and pushed the adjusted bar up on me.\n\nTop singles (best sign): f10 (0.99), f8 (0.76), f38 short (0.68), f30 (0.54), f21 (0.50).\n\n### Why I trust this one\n\nThe combination behaves like three genuinely independent signals stacking, not like a lucky interaction:\n\n- Each leg is strong standalone. The triple didn't conjure performance out of features that were individually worthless \u2014 that's the failure mode that reliably dies out of sample.\n- Sign flips are decisively punished: 1.396 \u2192 0.62 / 0.53 / 0.26 for the three single-flips. The orientation is identified by real structure, not noise.\n- The lift from pair (1.230) to triple (1.396) is the modest, diminishing gain you'd expect from adding a correlated-but-distinct third signal, not a suspicious jump.\n\n### Why I haircut to 1.05\n\nThe 1.396 is an upward-biased estimate and shouldn't be taken at face value. I selected the max over ~70 evaluations, and the components were themselves each chosen as top performers out of 40 \u2014 so bias compounds at both levels. Pure sampling error at n=5000 is only ~0.15, so the bulk of my 0.30 sd is selection bias and regime uncertainty, not estimation noise. The 25% haircut reflects that; the wide sd acknowledges I can't verify from in-sample data alone whether any leg is quietly regime-dependent.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: all of this is in-sample evidence about specification *structure*. I have no way to test stability across sub-periods with the tools available, so if the true OOS period differs in regime, the realized number could fall toward the lower end of my interval."}
